WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.600
When Disney fans think "Jungle Cruise," they think&nbsp;
riding around in boats, fascinating animals, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.960
punny dad-jokes galore. All that can be found in&nbsp;
Disney's new film based on the ride, but there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.580
also so much more. Here's everything only adults&nbsp;
are likely to notice in Disney's "Jungle Cruise."

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.960
Adults who remember the music of the&nbsp;
early 1990s will be surprised when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.600
immediately recognize the first few notes of&nbsp;
the ballad that opens "Jungle Cruise." No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.160
that song wasn't composed&nbsp;
specifically for the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.800
it's the instrumental to power ballad "Nothing&nbsp;
Else Matters," which was released by Metallica in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:39.029
1991 and has since become a staple of the band's&nbsp;
live shows and one of its most recognizable songs.

00:00:39.029 --> 00:00:42.240
According to the credits of "Jungle&nbsp;
Cruise," Metallica reimagined "Nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.440
Else Matters" with the film's&nbsp;
composer James Newton Howard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.560
and the four current members of the band -&nbsp;
James Hetfield, Lars Ulrich, Kirk Hammett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.120
and Robert Trujillo - are listed as featured&nbsp;
performers on the movie's version of the song.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.360
The ballad's haunting, melancholy&nbsp;
quality makes it especially recognizable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.560
and while the melody is lovely,&nbsp;
it's not clear why Howard decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.640
to feature an orchestral version of a&nbsp;
hit from the '90s in the film. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.040
for those in the know, the inclusion of "Nothing&nbsp;
Else Matters" may be downright distracting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:16.480
especially given the movie takes place in 1916&nbsp;
and otherwise features an entirely original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.330
score. On the other hand, it could also serve&nbsp;
as a good excuse to introduce kids to Metallica.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.533
From a bus full of soldiers to German U-Boats,&nbsp;
historical references abound in "Jungle Cruise."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:32.080
However, unless they're exceptionally young&nbsp;
history buffs, kids are unlikely to be familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.960
with the film's numerous nods to World War I.&nbsp;
After all, while World War II's Nazis remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.840
popular film and TV villains, it's much rarer&nbsp;
for Germans from World War I to be a story's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:47.040
antagonist, as is the case with Jesse Plemons'&nbsp;
villainous Prince Joachim in "Jungle Cruise."

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.280
Part of Lily and MacGregor Houghton's argument&nbsp;
for going after the Tears of the Moon is that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.320
could revolutionize medicine by healing British&nbsp;
and other Allied soldiers, who were even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.634
likely to die of disease in the trenches&nbsp;
than they were to be killed by the Germans.

00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:05.200
The film also references Kaiser Wilhelm&nbsp;
II, the last emperor of Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.080
who abdicated his throne upon losing the&nbsp;
war in 1918. However, even adults may not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.320
know that Wilhelm's youngest son really was&nbsp;
named Joachim. As far as we know, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.090
the real-life Prince Joachim never took a U-Boat&nbsp;
up the Amazon to search for a legendary tree.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.301
Throughout the movie, Lily encounters&nbsp;
more than her fair share of overt sexism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.301 --> 00:02:28.400
She's first introduced in the balcony of a&nbsp;
theater where her brother is presenting a speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.680
she clearly wrote to an academic guild made up&nbsp;
entirely of men. And even though she has a Ph.D.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.896
her knowledge and abilities are often questioned,&nbsp;
as is her resolve to go after the Tears of the&nbsp;Moon. 

00:02:38.896 --> 00:02:43.040
Even her brother worries about who would&nbsp;
take care of her if she went to the Amazon alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.320
although she's clearly more than&nbsp;
capable of taking care of herself.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.680
In addition, her choice to wear pants, a&nbsp;
rarity in 1916, is called out repeatedly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.240
Frank finds her choice to be so exceptional&nbsp;
that he even gives her the nickname "Pants."

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.440
Kids these days may be surprised by the treatment&nbsp;
the obviously smart and determined Lily endures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.360
Adults, however, will know quite well that&nbsp;
blatant discrimination against women was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.000
more or less the global standard, making her&nbsp;
the story's underdog just by being a woman.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.480
Soon after Frank and Lily first meet,&nbsp;
Frank is revealed to have lied to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.400
about his true identity. However, he&nbsp;
regains her confidence when he goes up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.480
against a jaguar who menaces the patrons&nbsp;
at the restaurant where they're dining.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.520
After all, if Frank can take on a jaguar, he's&nbsp;
certainly capable of taking her up the Amazon.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.527
"Careful. They can smell fear."

00:03:29.527 --> 00:03:33.680
However, Lily missed one thing: Jaguars&nbsp;
and other animals are unlikely to attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.640
humans unless they've been directly threatened,&nbsp;
which this jaguar clearly was not. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.280
many adults in the audience may realize that&nbsp;
since the jaguar wasn't acting in self-defense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:48.573
it's unclear why it came into the restaurant, much&nbsp;
less attacked the large gathering of people there.

00:03:48.573 --> 00:03:51.680
Given the fear and adrenaline Lily&nbsp;
might have felt in that moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.200
she seems to have failed to notice that&nbsp;
given those variables, while Frank's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.240
ability to wrestle the jaguar seemed impressive,&nbsp;
something else was clearly going on. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.720
the movie soon reveals that Frank and the&nbsp;
jaguar, who's named Proxima, are in cahoots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.671
with Proxima attacking Frank in order&nbsp;
to secure him the job as Lily's skipper.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.080
Early in their trip down the Amazon, Frank's&nbsp;
boat La Quila sails past a pod of long-peaked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.400
dolphins that all bear little resemblance to&nbsp;
the bottlenose dolphins many people are most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.040
familiar with. However, adults may be aware&nbsp;
that there are numerous dolphin species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.600
and one of them is the Amazon river dolphin&nbsp;
shown in the movie. This freshwater dolphin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.320
is native to the Amazon and is also sometimes&nbsp;
referred to as the pink river dolphin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.600
or in Brazil, the boto cor de&nbsp;
rosa due to its pinkish hue.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.960
In the movie, Lily refers to the&nbsp;
dolphins by their scientific name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.160
inia geoffrensis. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
Frank's identification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:47.040
of them as "encantado" evokes a myth about&nbsp;
Amazon river dolphins being shape-shifters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.080
and more broadly refers to the many other legends&nbsp;
surrounding the species, including the one Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.911
relates: that looking into a pink river dolphins'&nbsp;
eyes will lead to a lifetime of nightmares.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.880
Almost 20 years ago, another movie inspired by&nbsp;
a boat-based Disney theme park ride premiered:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.640
"Pirates of the Caribbean: The Curse of&nbsp;
the Black Pearl." You may have heard of it.

00:05:07.131 --> 00:05:08.003
"Savvy?"

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.320
While many younger children are&nbsp;
unlikely to have seen that 2003 film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.720
adults may get a sense of deja vu when watching&nbsp;
"Jungle Cruise" due to the fact that the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.280
supernatural storyline largely overlaps&nbsp;
with that of the earlier movie. Much like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.720
the pirates who were cursed with eternal&nbsp;
life after they took the wrong treasure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.640
the conquistadors who went after the Tears&nbsp;
of the Moon in "Jungle Cruise" were cursed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:33.680
with eternal life for attempting to take that&nbsp;
treasure. Inevitably, just like Captain Barbossa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.840
and his crew in "Pirates," Aguirre and his&nbsp;
allies wish to break their immortal bonds.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:42.320
If that weren't enough, the mid-movie revelation&nbsp;
that Frank himself is also cursed with unending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.440
life because 400 years ago he was Aguirre's&nbsp;
map maker parallels the eventual revelation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.480
"Pirates" that Jack Sparrow is undead because he&nbsp;
was the captain of the Black Pearl before Barbossa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:57.280
and his crew mutinied. The resemblance between&nbsp;
the "Jungle Cruise" and "Pirates" mythology may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.600
raise some adults' eyebrows, not to mention make&nbsp;
them wonder why the powers that be at Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.680
felt the need to include a supernatural&nbsp;
plotline in a film based on a ride that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:10.080
unlike "Pirates," never included anything&nbsp;
that could be interpreted as supernatural.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.360
"Jungle Cruise" nods to several well-known&nbsp;
films that only adults are likely to notice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.760
In addition to "Pirates of the Caribbean,"&nbsp;
there are hat tips to 1999's "The Mummy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:23.040
the "Indiana Jones" films, and&nbsp;
"Romancing the Stone." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.634
if there's one classic that especially influenced&nbsp;
the new film, it's 1951's "The African Queen."

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:32.000
Theme park buffs will know the "Jungle&nbsp;
Cruise" homage to the Humphrey Bogart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.640
and Katharine Hepburn-led classic is no&nbsp;
accident; Walt Disney was partially inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:40.400
to create the Jungle Cruise attraction&nbsp;
in Disneyland by "The African Queen."

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.920
Given that connection, it's not surprising&nbsp;
that the new movie bears a noticeable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:48.080
resemblance to the older film. Both have a&nbsp;
plot that revolves around a skipper becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.720
involved with a brother and sister who require&nbsp;
passage down a waterway during World War I.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.880
"My brother and I are&nbsp;
looking for passage upriver."

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:55.724
"Please go away."

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:58.002
"I have a lot of money."

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.040
"The African Queen" influence can also be&nbsp;
seen in the dynamic between Frank and Lily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.960
who constantly butt heads only to come&nbsp;
to respect and then fall for one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.120
just like Bogart and Hepburn's characters.&nbsp;
Adults who notice the connection between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.160
two films may use it as an opportunity to&nbsp;
introduce their kids to the 1951 classic.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.680
While Disney has attempted to be more inclusive&nbsp;
by incorporating more openly gay characters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:24.960
films like the live-action remakes of&nbsp;
"Beauty and the Beast" and "Cruella,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:29.360
it's done so in such a subtle way that&nbsp;
plenty of people wouldn't even notice.

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:33.200
Unfortunately, "Jungle Cruise" has the&nbsp;
same issue. The film includes perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.200
the most overtly "out" character yet in the&nbsp;
form of Lily's clothes-obsessed brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.520
MacGregor. However, it tiptoes lightly&nbsp;
around the issue of his sexuality.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.360
Toward the middle of the film, MacGregor&nbsp;
confesses to Frank that although he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.240
share his sister's belief in the&nbsp;
legend of the Tears of the Moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.240
he sticks by Lily because unlike everyone&nbsp;
else in his life, she didn't abandon him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.960
when she learned about his sexuality. However,&nbsp;
the whole story is told through implication;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.400
while adults will likely pick up on&nbsp;
what MacGregor's saying, his wording&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.887
is vague enough that many kids are unlikely to&nbsp;
realize what exactly he's even talking about.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.680
In the film's third act, Lily is forced to&nbsp;
swim for the first time after Frank finds an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:13.693
underwater chamber with a lever in it that&nbsp;
will lead them to the Tears of the Moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:17.520
What kids and even some adults may not realize&nbsp;
is that when Frank kisses Lily for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.760
time while she's trapped underwater, it's&nbsp;
not for any romantic reason. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:25.360
given the pair's will-they-or-won't-they&nbsp;
dynamic throughout the story, the kiss can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:29.680
easily be interpreted as the characters finally&nbsp;
admitting their feelings for one another.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:33.920
However, that's not Frank's intention. As&nbsp;
he pantomimes, he's simply trying to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.000
Lily with air so she can stay underwater&nbsp;
long enough to pull the chamber's lever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.760
Of course, even adults would be justified&nbsp;
in interpreting Frank as having something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.640
of an ulterior motive here, especially since&nbsp;
the exchange of oxygen between the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:49.570
looks more like a smooch than&nbsp;
mouth-to-mouth resuscitation.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.640
Kids who've been lucky enough to visit a Disney&nbsp;
park may pick up on some of the film's direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.360
references to the Jungle Cruise ride. When&nbsp;
Frank goes under a makeshift waterfall early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.680
in the film and triumphantly introduces "the&nbsp;
backside of water" with a declaration that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:08.173
it's "the eighth wonder of the world," everyone&nbsp;
familiar with the ride will smile knowingly.

00:09:08.173 --> 00:09:11.680
However, there are some less obvious nods&nbsp;
to the attraction that kids may miss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:17.120
including a shocking number of ridiculous new&nbsp;
puns and an early reference to Dr. Albert Falls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:20.134
the man the ride's waterfall&nbsp;
is supposedly named after.

00:09:20.216 --> 00:09:23.200
"The rocks you see here in&nbsp;
the river are sandstone, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.244
some people just take them for granite."

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.560
Plus, when Frank is first introduced guiding&nbsp;
a jungle cruise of tourists down the Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:35.760
he essentially functions as a skipper in the theme&nbsp;
park ride. As his boat continues along the water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:39.680
it's revealed that Frank has rigged up his&nbsp;
own makeshift Jungle Cruise attraction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.160
complete with a fake hippo,&nbsp;
in order to provide the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:46.106
thrilling -- and silly -- experience&nbsp;
as the ride Disney Parks fans adore.

00:09:46.106 --> 00:09:49.440
"It's one of my&nbsp;
boulder attractions." 

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.120
Interestingly, after delays due to the&nbsp;
coronavirus pandemic, "Jungle Cruise"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.120
is hitting theaters just as Disneyland&nbsp;
opens an updated version of the ride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.280
and the revised experience no longer includes&nbsp;
characters that are featured in the film.

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:04.880
After years of complaints over the&nbsp;
attraction's depictions of native people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.880 --> 00:10:09.920
Disneyland removed some of the audio-animatronic&nbsp;
characters that appear toward the end of the ride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:14.720
including Trader Sam, who is willing to exchange&nbsp;
"two of his shrunken heads for one of yours."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.560
The updated ride also excised a group&nbsp;
of headhunters who are characterized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.200
as savages and throw spears at the ride vehicle.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.960
Yet "Jungle Cruise" features both a&nbsp;
gender-swapped Trader Sam and a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.760
of supposedly threatening Indigenous people.&nbsp;
While the story avoids shading into racism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.200
by revealing that the natives' behavior is&nbsp;
all just part of a scheme cooked up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:37.120
Frank to make his jungle cruises more&nbsp;
exciting, adults who keep up on Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:41.520
parks news will know that these references&nbsp;
to the ride are now things of the past.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.440
"Well, I look forward to disappointing you."

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.840
Check out one of our newest&nbsp;
videos right here! Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.160
even more Looper videos about your favorite&nbsp;
movies and TV shows are coming soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.534
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit&nbsp;
the bell so you don't miss a single one.

